function domain(m){const w=[...new Set(m.writers.filter(x=>x.startsWith('services/')).map(x=>x.split('/')[1]))];if(w.length)return w.join(' / ');if(/^(User|Account|Session|Verification|Password|PublicIdentity)/.test(m.name))return 'identity/security';if(/Agent/.test(m.name))return 'agents';if(/Payroll|Hris|Employee/.test(m.name))return 'HRIS/payroll';if(/Journal|Accounting|Ledger|Accountant|FiscalYear|Close/.test(m.name))return 'accounting/assurance';if(/Inventory|Stock|Item|Unit|Brand|Category/.test(m.name))return 'inventory/catalog';if(/Purchase|Supplier|GoodsReceipt/.test(m.name))return 'purchasing';if(/Payment|Reconciliation|Statement|Suspense|MatchRecord|Provider/.test(m.name))return 'payments/reconciliation';return 'platform/cross-domain — owner validation required';}
write('model-ownership-register.csv',csv(models.map(m=>({model:m.name,businessPurpose:m.name.replace(/([a-z])([A-Z])/g,'$1 $2')+' persisted record; label inferred from schema, detailed behavior in domain assessment',owningDomainCandidate:domain(m),authorityStatus:'Candidate from direct call inventory + naming; not exclusive-write proof',writerCandidates:m.writers.filter(x=>x.startsWith('services/')||x.startsWith('lib/')),otherWriterCandidates:m.writers.filter(x=>!x.startsWith('services/')&&!x.startsWith('lib/')),tenantBoundary:m.directTenant?'organizationId present; composite relation count '+m.tenantRelations:'Inherited parent or global catalog: verify caller scope; not automatically a defect',lifecycle:m.lifecycle.length?m.lifecycle:'No parsed status/effective/version field; service lifecycle must be reviewed',retention:'No model-specific approved retention duration established; preserve financial/statutory/HR evidence pending qualified policy',deletionShape:m.cascades.length?m.cascades:'No parsed cascading relation',evidence:'prisma/schema.prisma:'+m.startLine,status:'Systematic static inventory; authority/retention confidence limited'}))));

write('schema-assessment.md',`# Schema and integrity assessment\n\n## Scope and current state\n\nThe complete Prisma model set and all local migration files were parsed: **211 models, 87 migrations**. The configured database resolved to a local PostgreSQL target. Catalog and aggregate queries ran in explicit READ ONLY transactions, with rollback, statement/lock timeouts and no business identifiers in results. This is local deployed-schema evidence, not production evidence.\n\nEvery model has an entry in ${report('schema-model-inventory.csv')} and ${report('model-ownership-register.csv')}. The former records fields, decimals, JSON, candidate writes/reads, lifecycle and cascades; the latter assigns a candidate purpose/domain, tenant boundary and explicit authority/retention confidence. ${report('verification/schema-model-details.json')} preserves the field-level inventory and source lines. Regex candidates exclude dynamic calls/raw SQL and are **not proof of exclusive authority**. A reviewed model-specific retention schedule was not established; the register does not invent one.\n\n## Intended versus local database\n\n- All 211 model tables and scalar/enum/array column names have local catalog counterparts. No missing or extra model columns were detected.\n- All 87 migration names and SHA-256 checksums match recorded applied migrations; no unfinished or pending migration was found.\n- Five additional tables are accounted for: _UserRoles, _prisma_migrations, legacy_production_batches, legacy_recipe_ingredients, legacy_recipes. Legacy manufacturing tables are not evidence of an active BOM/production module.\n- The catalog contains 216 tables, 55 noninternal triggers and 147 CHECK constraints.\n- This comparison establishes table/column-name and migration-history parity. It does **not** prove exhaustive type/default/index/privilege parity, fresh replay, safe upgrades or production drift absence. Catalog definitions were retained for those next checks.\n\nEvidence: ${report('verification/schema-database-comparison.json')}; ${report('verification/database-metadata.json')}; ${report('verification/migration-inventory.json')}. Prisma validation passed. No migrations or seed scripts ran during this audit.\n\n## Authority, identity and retention\n\nMost business models carry organizationId. The 28 without it include authentication/global catalogs and child rows such as InventoryLevel, order lines, cash drawers and agent steps. Parent scoping can be correct; absence of organizationId is not a vulnerability by itself. Newer purchase-correction, fiscal and payroll evidence uses stronger composite tenant links and immutable lifecycle controls. Older journals, AP and several child relations still depend more heavily on service validation.\n\n${C.ap} checks supplier/PO scope and locks economic owners. ${C.hris} resolves employee organization and effective manager/delegation scope; its legacy location-manager fallback is current-only. Financial audit data and payroll/private HR data have different access needs; model-specific purge/legal-hold policies and actual scheduler enforcement remain unverified. Cascading organization ownership must not become an accidental financial-record eraser. F10/F14 explain the bounded risks without claiming a live exploit.\n\nIdentifiers in JSON/sourceType/sourceId fields are flexible but do not gain relational integrity automatically. The inventory lists unlinked ID-like fields and JSON fields. Their business validity must be demonstrated by source-owner commands, schema versions, hashes and migration/repair tooling. In particular, SupplierInvoice.ledgerPostingBatchId is a nullable string: link coverage must distinguish legitimate drafts from incorrectly unposted final documents.\n\n## Money, quantities and time\n\nThe parsed schema uses Decimal for money/quantities and no Float fields. Common quantities use Decimal(12,3), unit costs Decimal(12,2), totals Decimal(14,2). This avoids binary floating-point storage but still requires a reviewed rounding policy at each calculation boundary. Source amounts, tax, base amounts, rate direction and currency version must be separate immutable facts: F01/F07 show that field availability alone does not preserve meaning.\n\nInventoryTransaction carries signed quantity, balanceAfter, effectiveAt, recordedAt and timeProvenance. These support business-time and recorded-time analysis. Legacy-createdAt approximation is explicitly represented. Backdated valuation and cutoff behavior still need isolated end-to-end tests. Partial fiscal-month clipping is defective (F08). Analytics order-activity derives organization timezone/currency and half-open windows; do not generalize that correctness to every report.\n\n## Transactions, concurrency and derived data\n\nThe inventory stock-event kernel uses versioned conditional updates and available-quantity checks; AP commands lock supplier/PO rows and record durable posting/recovery evidence. Payroll transitions use expected versions, serializable transactions and immutable snapshots/events. Period posting obtains a shared row lock, while close obtains an exclusive lock. Outboxes use bounded claims, retry counts, stale leases and dead-letter states. These are positive design facts, but mocks cannot establish PostgreSQL race behavior.\n\nOffline queue operations lack an equivalent transactional durability boundary (F03/F04). Generic accounting reversal omits some source ownership (F02). A period lock and a current close-assurance readiness decision are not interchangeable (F06).\n\n## Read-only consistency results and denominators\n\n| Check | Observed local result | Limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Journal balance | 0 unbalanced POSTED/REVERSED entry-currency groups | Balanced does not mean economically complete or correct in base currency. |\n| Journal tenant joins | 0 line/entry/account tenant mismatches | No adversarial inserts or all-relation tenant proof. |\n| FX storage | 12 non-unit-rate lines, 0 candidate conversion mismatches | Current service reproduction still fails F01; sample may be seeded. |\n| Journal source linkage | 4 posted/reversed entries lack postingBatchId | Local observation requiring fixture/source classification, not a production-corruption claim. |\n| Stock projection | 541 levels; 0 available/on-hand/reserved mismatches; 0 negatives; 0 approximate value mismatches | Does not prove all valuation policy scenarios. |\n| Stock movements | 541 levels all have movement evidence; signed physical movement sums agree with quantities | Opening-seed lineage and inventory-to-GL valuation completeness still need controlled proof. |\n| AP | 24 allocations with consistent tenant/supplier/currency; no overallocated draft payment | All 24 invoices and 24 payments are DRAFT; zero posted AP lifecycle denominator. |\n| Payroll | 24 run arithmetic checks agree | All 24 runs are DRAFT (with payslips); zero emitted/posted denominator. Earlier zero emitted mismatch count is therefore non-probative. |\n| Reconciliation | 24 runs | All DRAFT; no certificate hashes or sign-off. |\n| Periods | 0 overlapping pairs or out-of-year periods | Does not cover F08 creation edge case. |\n\nThe sampled ledger contains opening balances, POS sale/payment and stock adjustment batches. It cannot prove purchase/payroll/reconciliation source-to-ledger completeness. Query text and aggregate outputs: ${report('verification/database-tieouts.json')}, ${report('verification/database-cross-domain-tieouts.json')}.\n\n## Performance, migration and recovery limits\n\nTenant/time/status indexes are present across the major fact tables, and the order-activity report uses full database aggregation rather than a truncated application slice. No EXPLAIN ANALYZE, large-tenant load test, query-count budget or production latency/cost study was run. Therefore no scalability or cost readiness is certified.\n\nHistorical local restore evidence covers 80 migrations on 29 August, not the current 87-migration runtime. Current fresh replay, upgrade from a populated prior release, trigger negative tests, recovery objectives and actual restore/login/accounting checks remain explicit release work. No rollback script should be inferred from Prisma migration presence.`);

write('verification/test-failure-register.json',JSON.stringify(tests.flatMap(t=>t.testResults.filter(s=>s.status==='failed').map(s=>({group:t.file,suite:s.name,assertions:s.assertionResults.filter(a=>a.status==='failed').map(a=>({title:a.fullName,messages:a.failureMessages})),message:s.message}))),null,2));
write('verification-summary.md',`# Verification results\n\n## Existing unit/service/workflow tests\n\n| Group | Suites | Passed assertions | Failed assertions | Pending |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n${testTable}\n| Total (excluding duplicate smoke test) | ${totals.suites} | ${totals.passed} | ${totals.failed} | ${totals.pending} |\n\nThe initial queue smoke test passed 3 assertions; it is excluded from totals because the commerce cohort contains the same test. Test selections and complete JSON/stdout/stderr are retained in verification/. No new application tests or source fixes were added.\n\n**Runner limit:** normal Jest/nextJest startup attempts timed out without results. The successful audit-only CJS adapter uses the repository jest.setup.ts, installed Next SWC transformer, relevant roots, alias/server-only/font/style mappings and jsdom. It bypasses asynchronous Next configuration discovery. These results are useful current unit evidence, **not an exact CI pass**. DATABASE_URL and related variables target inaccessible localhost:1 for command checks; actual PostgreSQL/integration/mutating scripts were excluded. Prisma mocks are not database atomicity proof. The direct adapter is saved alongside the reports.\n\n## Failure triage\n\n- Accountant work desk: mock does not provide closeAssuranceFinding.findMany now queried by the service. This prevents intended assertions from reaching behavior.\n- Manual journals: mock expects query.sql, while the current period lock uses a tagged raw query. The failure is the mock, not proof that live balanced journals throw this TypeError.\n- Payroll backfill plan/execution/reconciliation: mocks lack current module configuration/grant dependencies. No actual backfill was run.\n- Payroll statutory scenario tests: expected CNPS READY contradicts the current SUPPORTED_DRAFT capability gate. Preserve the gate pending qualified review; update fixtures/contracts intentionally.\n- Business Pulse links: inventory.read versus destination-specific inventory.items.read expectation needs contract reconciliation. Current resolver checks requiredPermission; this is not evidence of unauthorized access.\n- Any supplementary failures are retained verbatim in ${report('verification/test-failure-register.json')}; do not classify a new failure as harmless without reviewing it.\n\n## Other commands and probes\n\n- Prisma validation: passed.\n- Full TypeScript no-emit check: timed out after 420 seconds, with no completed result. Not passed.\n- Eight structural gates passed: inventory boundary, inventory valuation truth, ledger close truth, payment cash truth, purchasing AP consolidation, offline replay, payroll presence, service boundary.\n- Payroll trust-spine gate: blocked (5/6 ready), exact regression-text/persistence marker check. Transition behavior tests still ran in the people suite.\n- Report-trust gate: blocked (30/35 ready). Failing clauses: service-owned currency, trust banner wiring, settlement reversal foundation, signed statement external access foundation, consented referral launch foundation. The first two inspect literal source strings and can be stale; the remaining clauses require explicit reconciliation with current contracts. No blanket false-positive dismissal.\n- Audit behavior probes: seven current accounting behaviors and three offline queue conditions reproduced. Synthetic doubles isolate logic; authorization allowed in accounting probes, no live writes.\n- Local database: metadata parity, migration hashes and aggregate tie-outs completed in READ ONLY transactions; raw business identifiers were not saved.\n- Browser: attempted hidden in-app navigation to http://localhost:3000/en returned ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED; no Node listening service was identified in the local check. No authenticated workflow, visual, screen-reader, keyboard or responsive-mobile test was completed.\n- No current provider/authority sandbox, deploy, load, worker schedule, backup restore or mutating PostgreSQL concurrency test ran.\n\nEvery command wrapper records command, timestamp, exit/signal, timeout and sanitized database target in *.result.json. Historical runs and failed startup attempts remain preserved; none were overwritten to obtain a pass.`);

write('external-source-register.md',`# External source provenance\n\nRetrieved/checked 2026-09-30. External pages establish publication/scope context only; no legal, tax or accounting certification is claimed.\n\n| Source | Observation and limit |\n|---|---|\n| [OHADA AUDCIF publication](https://www.ohada.org/publication-du-nouvel-acte-uniforme-relatif-au-droit-comptable-et-a-l-information-financiere-audcif/) | Official accounting-framework publication, useful for country-pack scope. Does not mean every member state shares payroll/tax rules or that this software complies. |\n| [Cameroon DGI 2026 Code général des impôts publication](https://www.impots.cm/en/node/1333) | Official page identifies an edition updated 1 January 2026. Current pack cites CGI 2025; refresh provenance and perform qualified delta review. This audit did not independently recalculate statutory rates from the full code. |\n| [CNPS employer obligations](https://www.cnps.cm/fr/employeurs/obligations-de-lemployeur1.html) | Official search result located; direct page retrieval failed. Not used as a basis for any precise rate, deadline or legal conclusion. |\n\nRepository pack references and source-verification labels are recorded in cameroon.ts. SOURCE_CHECKED, SUPPORTED_DRAFT, PUBLISHED and independent qualified approval are distinct states.`);

